<p>A new set of reports describes 2025 as a notable milestone for the global power system: clean electricity grew fast enough to meet 100% of the world’s additional electricity demand, so fossil-fuel generation did not need to rise overall. Together, the reports point to a broader structural shift in the power sector, with analysts and researchers saying rapid deployment of solar and other renewables is increasingly changing how countries meet rising electricity needs. One of the clearest signs of that shift was coal’s changing role. A report cited by multiple outlets said renewables overtook coal generation globally in 2025, with solar growth as the main driver. That matters beyond climate targets because it suggests many countries now have a stronger domestic alternative to imported oil and gas at a time when energy security remains a major concern.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Solar driver: The reporting identifies solar as the main force behind the rise in renewable generation that pushed clean power above coal in 2025.</li><li>Energy security: SCMP says the findings came amid renewed concerns about energy security, strengthening the case for domestic clean power as an alternative to imported fuels.</li><li>Structural shift: The Financial Times says analysts see growing evidence that the move away from fossil fuels in electricity generation is structural rather than temporary.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Ember: The energy think tank’s report says 2025 marked a turning point because clean power grew faster than electricity demand, preventing a rise in fossil generation. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/economy/global-economy/article/3350759/turning-point-clean-energy-met-100-worlds-new-power-needs-2025-report'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Analysts cited by Financial Times: They say the latest data increasingly support the view that global electricity systems are moving through a lasting transition away from fossil fuels. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/d525d8ea-9a07-4e22-8780-48bae3fdf2bb'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>Climate-focused reporting in The Straits Times: Its coverage emphasizes that cutting coal generation remains essential to meeting global climate targets, making the 2025 crossover especially significant. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/solar-growth-drives-global-renewables-to-overtake-coal-power-in-2025-report-shows'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/d525d8ea-9a07-4e22-8780-48bae3fdf2bb'>Global clean power growth points to permanent shift, analysts say</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/economy/global-economy/article/3350759/turning-point-clean-energy-met-100-worlds-new-power-needs-2025-report'>Turning point? Clean energy met 100% of world’s new power needs in 2025: report</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/solar-growth-drives-global-renewables-to-overtake-coal-power-in-2025-report-shows'>Solar growth drives global renewables to overtake coal power in 2025, report shows</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reddit.com/r/worldnews/comments/1sraoio/solar_and_wind_outpace_coal_as_energy_crisis/'>Solar and wind outpace coal as energy crisis fails to spark fossil fuel revival</a> - reddit.com</li></ul>